
  <sn><md>### Polysaccharides

- Polysaccharides are large polymers of the monosaccharides(&gt;10). 
- Unlike monosaccharides and disaccharides, polysaccharides are either `insoluble` or form `colloidal suspensions`. 
- The polysaccharides found in nature either serve a `structural` function (**structural polysaccharides**) or play a role as a `stored form of energy` (**storage polysaccharides**).

**Storage Polysaccharides** (Digestible Polysaccharides)

The principal storage polysaccharides are `STARCH(P) AND GLYCOGEN(A)`. 
Starch is a polymer of alpha glucose and is, in fact, a mixture of two different polysaccharides – `AMYLOSE AND AMYLOPECTIN`.
</md></sn>
